    "gold_solution": "```python\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Disaster_Response_Logistics\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 300)  # Set time limit to 300 seconds\n\n    # Data\n    depots = list(range(3))  # D0..D2\n    units = list(range(4))  # U0..U3\n    supplies = list(range(2))  # S0..S1\n    days = list(range(1))  # Day0..Day0\n\n    demands = [[[40.41], [49.39]], [[18.89], [31.45]], [[10.63], [38.65]], [[11.73], [29.76]]]\n    depot_supplies = [[[26.76], [58.97]], [[27.33], [62.12]], [[25.61], [48.93]]]\n    transport_costs = [[4.87, 1.18, 1.33, 2.64], [4.63, 2.79, 3.81, 1.88], [3.83, 2.65, 2.0, 3.8]]\n    risk_costs = [[133.01, 99.72, 140.55, 112.92], [162.22, 108.37, 80.72, 123.29], [105.74, 102.04, 138.01, 139.2]]\n    supply_volumes = [0.6, 1.78]\n    penalty_costs = [97.66, 76.02]\n    vehicle_cost = 10.0\n    vehicle_capacity = 100.0\n    total_vehicles = 20\n\n    # Decision variables\n    # Flow_D{d}_U{u}_S{s}_Day{t}: Amount of supply s from depot d to unit u on day t\n    flows = {}\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for u in units:\n                for s in supplies:\n                    flows[(d, u, s, t)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Flow_D{d}_U{u}_S{s}_Day{t}\")\n\n    # Slack_U{u}_S{s}_Day{t}: Shortage of supply s at unit u on day t\n    slacks = {}\n    for t in days:\n        for u in units:\n            for s in supplies:\n                slacks[(u, s, t)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Slack_U{u}_S{s}_Day{t}\")\n\n    # Veh_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}: Number of vehicles on route d->u on day t\n    vehicles = {}\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for u in units:\n                vehicles[(d, u, t)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.INTEGER, name=f\"Veh_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}\")\n\n    # Route_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}: Route d->u is active on day t (binary)\n    routes = {}\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for u in units:\n                routes[(d, u, t)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Route_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}\")\n\n    # Objective: Minimize total cost (transport + penalty + vehicle + risk)\n    obj = quicksum(\n        transport_costs[d][u] * flows[(d, u, s, t)]\n        for t in days for d in depots for u in units for s in supplies\n    )\n    obj += quicksum(\n        penalty_costs[s] * slacks[(u, s, t)]\n        for t in days for u in units for s in supplies\n    )\n    obj += quicksum(\n        vehicle_cost * vehicles[(d, u, t)]\n        for t in days for d in depots for u in units\n    )\n    obj += quicksum(\n        risk_costs[d][u] * routes[(d, u, t)]\n        for t in days for d in depots for u in units\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Depot capacity constraints: Total outflow <= supply\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for s in supplies:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    quicksum(flows[(d, u, s, t)] for u in units) <= depot_supplies[d][s][t],\n                    name=f\"DepotCap_D{d}_S{s}_Day{t}\"\n                )\n\n    # Demand constraints: Inflow + slack >= demand\n    for t in days:\n        for u in units:\n            for s in supplies:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    quicksum(flows[(d, u, s, t)] for d in depots) + slacks[(u, s, t)] >= demands[u][s][t],\n                    name=f\"Demand_U{u}_S{s}_Day{t}\"\n                )\n\n    # Vehicle capacity constraints: Total volume <= vehicles * capacity\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for u in units:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    quicksum(supply_volumes[s] * flows[(d, u, s, t)] for s in supplies) <= vehicle_capacity * vehicles[(d, u, t)],\n                    name=f\"VehCap_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}\"\n                )\n\n    # Route activation constraints: vehicles <= total_vehicles * route\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for u in units:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    vehicles[(d, u, t)] <= total_vehicles * routes[(d, u, t)],\n                    name=f\"RouteAct_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}\"\n                )\n\n    # Fleet size constraints: Total vehicles <= total_vehicles\n    for t in days:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(vehicles[(d, u, t)] for d in depots for u in units) <= total_vehicles,\n            name=f\"FleetSize_Day{t}\"\n        )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Situation:\nA severe weather event has disrupted the primary ground lines of communication across the riverine corridor. Bridge outages, debris, and intermittent security incidents are constraining movement from Supply Depot Alpha (airhead), Supply Depot Bravo (port zone), and Supply Depot Charlie (inland warehouse). Receiving elements\u2014Forward Operating Base Delta, Field Hospital Echo, Community Distribution Point Foxtrot, and Camp Golf\u2014report critical shortfalls in life-sustaining commodities. Several lateral routes remain passable but require escort and route clearance. Airlift is intermittent due to low ceilings; ground convoys remain the main effort for today\u2019s operational window.\n\nMission:\nDeliver priority relief commodities from available depots to the designated receiving units within the current operational period, minimizing total operational cost while preserving force protection and ensuring maximum humanitarian effect.\n\nExecution/Concept:\n- Demand and shortfalls: Each receiving unit has defined requirements by supply type. Unmet demand is permitted only as a last resort and will be recorded as controlled shortage (slack) with associated penalty costs. The objective function penalizes these shortfalls to encourage fulfillment wherever feasible.\n- Depot constraints: Outbound shipments for each supply are limited by on-hand inventory at each depot. Dispatchers will not allocate more of any item than is available.\n- Transport costs: Movement costs vary by depot\u2013unit pair and supply type, reflecting distance, terrain, and handling complexity.\n- Vehicle and capacity: Each dispatched vehicle incurs a deployment cost and can carry a limited volume. Cargo loading is constrained by volumetric factors per supply item. Vehicle dispatch counts must respect the fleet ceiling across all routes for the period.\n- Route activation and risk: No vehicles may operate on a depot\u2013unit corridor unless that route is explicitly activated. Activating a route triggers a fixed Risk Cost for security overhead (escort teams, route clearance, comms relay), charged once per route for the period, regardless of the number of vehicles sent. Consolidate flows to minimize the number of activated routes where tactically acceptable.\n- Security and access: Valley corridors are exposed to sporadic interference and may close without notice. Convoys should prioritize more secure spurs where activation yields acceptable risk\u2013benefit. If activation on a high-risk axis is essential to meet critical medical demand, planners must account for the fixed risk cost in the trade-space.\n- Prioritization: Life-saving medical and water/sanitation items are prioritized to clinical and high-density sites. When resources are insufficient, allocate to minimize shortage penalties while preserving routes that achieve the greatest volume efficiency per activated corridor.\n- Command and control: The Joint Logistics Operations Cell will confirm route status, greenlight activations, and allocate the limited fleet across corridors. Dispatchers will align loads to vehicle capacity and release convoys in blocks synchronized with security teams.\n\nAnnex A - Logistics Data:\nUnit Demands (Units x Supplies):\nUnit\\Supply | S 0 | S 1\n-----------------------\nU 0        |  40.41 |  49.39\nU 1        |  18.89 |  31.45\nU 2        |  10.63 |  38.65\nU 3        |  11.73 |  29.76\nDepot Supplies (Depots x Supplies):\nDepot\\Supply | S 0 | S 1\n------------------------\nD 0        |  26.76 |  58.97\nD 1        |  27.33 |  62.12\nD 2        |  25.61 |  48.93\nTransport Costs (Depot x Unit):\nDepot\\Unit | U 0 | U 1 | U 2 | U 3\n----------------------------------\nD 0        |   4.87 |   1.18 |   1.33 |   2.64\nD 1        |   4.63 |   2.79 |   3.81 |   1.88\nD 2        |   3.83 |   2.65 |   2.00 |   3.80\nRisk Costs (Depot x Unit):\nDepot\\Unit | U 0 | U 1 | U 2 | U 3\n----------------------------------\nD 0        | 133.01 |  99.72 | 140.55 | 112.92\nD 1        | 162.22 | 108.37 |  80.72 | 123.29\nD 2        | 105.74 | 102.04 | 138.01 | 139.20\nPenalty Costs per Unit Shortage: [Supply S0: 97.66, Supply S1: 76.02]\nVolume per Supply Unit: [Supply S0: 0.6, Supply S1: 1.78]\nFleet Parameters: Cost 10.0, Capacity 100.0, Total 20",
    "LLM_description_template": "Situation:\nA severe weather event has disrupted the primary ground lines of communication across the riverine corridor. Bridge outages, debris, and intermittent security incidents are constraining movement from Supply Depot Alpha (airhead), Supply Depot Bravo (port zone), and Supply Depot Charlie (inland warehouse). Receiving elements\u2014Forward Operating Base Delta, Field Hospital Echo, Community Distribution Point Foxtrot, and Camp Golf\u2014report critical shortfalls in life-sustaining commodities. Several lateral routes remain passable but require escort and route clearance. Airlift is intermittent due to low ceilings; ground convoys remain the main effort for today\u2019s operational window.\n\nMission:\nDeliver priority relief commodities from available depots to the designated receiving units within the current operational period, minimizing total operational cost while preserving force protection and ensuring maximum humanitarian effect.\n\nExecution/Concept:\n- Demand and shortfalls: Each receiving unit has defined requirements by supply type. Unmet demand is permitted only as a last resort and will be recorded as controlled shortage (slack) with associated penalty costs. The objective function penalizes these shortfalls to encourage fulfillment wherever feasible.\n- Depot constraints: Outbound shipments for each supply are limited by on-hand inventory at each depot. Dispatchers will not allocate more of any item than is available.\n- Transport costs: Movement costs vary by depot\u2013unit pair and supply type, reflecting distance, terrain, and handling complexity.\n- Vehicle and capacity: Each dispatched vehicle incurs a deployment cost and can carry a limited volume. Cargo loading is constrained by volumetric factors per supply item. Vehicle dispatch counts must respect the fleet ceiling across all routes for the period.\n- Route activation and risk: No vehicles may operate on a depot\u2013unit corridor unless that route is explicitly activated. Activating a route triggers a fixed Risk Cost for security overhead (escort teams, route clearance, comms relay), charged once per route for the period, regardless of the number of vehicles sent. Consolidate flows to minimize the number of activated routes where tactically acceptable.\n- Security and access: Valley corridors are exposed to sporadic interference and may close without notice. Convoys should prioritize more secure spurs where activation yields acceptable risk\u2013benefit. If activation on a high-risk axis is essential to meet critical medical demand, planners must account for the fixed risk cost in the trade-space.\n- Prioritization: Life-saving medical and water/sanitation items are prioritized to clinical and high-density sites. When resources are insufficient, allocate to minimize shortage penalties while preserving routes that achieve the greatest volume efficiency per activated corridor.\n- Command and control: The Joint Logistics Operations Cell will confirm route status, greenlight activations, and allocate the limited fleet across corridors. Dispatchers will align loads to vehicle capacity and release convoys in blocks synchronized with security teams.\n\nAnnex A - Logistics Data:\n{DEMANDS}\n{DEPOT_SUPPLIES}\n{TRANSPORT_COSTS}\n{RISK_COSTS}\n{PENALTY_COSTS}\n{SUPPLY_VOLUMES}\nFleet Parameters: Cost {VEHICLE_COST}, Capacity {VEHICLE_CAPACITY}, Total {TOTAL_VEHICLES}"
